// Practice quiz: Relationship pitfalls
// Unreviewed first draft, 2026-11-04. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/relationships-and-transactions/relationship-pitfalls

::o1 cascade remove::A parent mapping has CascadeType.REMOVE. What happens to children when the parent is deleted? {
=They are deleted too#Correct. The remove operation passes down.
~They stay with a null foreign key#Not quite. With the cascade they are deleted. Without it they are not removed automatically, and the parent delete can fail on the foreign-key constraint; a null key needs a nullable column and your own update.
~The delete fails always#Not quite. It normally succeeds.
~They move to another parent#Not quite. Nothing moves them.
}

::o1 orphan removal::What does orphanRemoval \= true react to? {
=A child being removed from the parent collection#Correct. It deletes the orphan row.
~A parent being read#Not quite. Reading has no effect.
~A new row being inserted#Not quite. That is cascade persist.
~A schema migration#Not quite. It is unrelated.
}

::o2 recursion::Why can serializing a bidirectional entity pair directly fail? {
=The JSON writer follows the cycle endlessly#Correct. Parent leads to child, and child leads back to parent.
~JSON cannot contain numbers#Not quite. JSON has numbers.
~Entities are private#Not quite. Visibility is not the cause.
~The database is locked#Not quite. The JSON writer loop is the cause.
}

::o2 DTO benefit::How does a DTO stop that cycle? {
=It contains only the fields you chose, with no back reference#Correct. The writer has nothing to follow.
~It encrypts the entity#Not quite. DTOs do not encrypt.
~It disables Jackson#Not quite. Serialization still happens.
~It makes queries lazy#Not quite. Fetching is separate.
}

::o3 toString::Why should toString not include a lazy collection? {
=A log line could run SQL or fail outside a session#Correct. Reading the collection triggers loading.
~Strings cannot be long#Not quite. They can.
~toString cannot be overridden#Not quite. It can.
~Hibernate deletes the collection#Not quite. It does not.
}

